Notes on the sketch of a normative ethic

    + argue for my safehold explanans here in the ethic (alone), where it figures as a premise
        : re `here in the ethic \(alone\)` cf. `so much for the aetiology: no need to argue here`
          @ ~/work/safehold/aetiology/._/text.notes_boneyard.brec
        - argue not on theoretic|epistemic grounds
            / chapter 1 of Waller 2020 convinces me there are none at present
            - nor on metaphysical grounds
                / contra Waller 2020, ∵ metaphysics mystifies me
        - rather argue on the practical|methodological ground of my own explanans
          being the best on which to premise a normative ethic
            - moreover the best on which to premise inferences of purpose and meaning in life
                : re `purpose and meaning in life`
                  N.B. `having as its purview.+the investigation of ${same}$`
                  @ ~/work/ethics/._/text.notes.brec
    - conditions of a moral law
        : note: these according to one or another of the various divine-command theories of morality,
          divorced of its supernatural assumptions, with the creator in place of God
        a) a command (addressed demand) of our creator
        b) the creator’s backing of it by force
    a) a command (addressed demand) of our creator
        : join @ `^*- conditions of a moral law$`
        ∵ reason addresses to us a demand (to endure)
        ∵ also this demand is proper to our creator
            ∵ our creator backs it by force
                : join `^*- the creator forces.+us.+to conform`
            / what more evidence [of the demand] could we expect?
        ∵ also the addressing of the demand is proper to our creator
            ∵ our creator advertises to us a reminder of the demand
                ∵ bright stars on every clear night advertise it
                ∵ this is by design of the creator
            / what more evidence [of the addressing] could we expect?
    b) the creator’s backing of it by force
        : join @ `^*- conditions of a moral law$`
        - the creator forces on us a tendency to conform (to endure)
            ∵ in creating us, the creator uses natural selection
            ∵ natural selection ingrains within us a tendency to endure
